public List<List<Integer>> permute(int[] nums) {
    List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
    if (nums.length == 0) {
        return result;
    }

    permute(nums, 0, new ArrayList<>(), result);
    return result;
}

private void permute(int[] nums, int start, List<Integer> current, List<List<Integer>> result) {
    if (start == nums.length) {
        result.add(new ArrayList<>(current));
        return;
    }

    for (int i = start; i < nums.length; i++) {
        current.add(nums[i]);
        permute(nums, i + 1, current, result);
        current.remove(current.size() - 1);
    }
}
```
This recursive solution uses backtracking to try all possible permutations of the array. The `permute` method takes an integer array `nums`, a start index `start`, and a current permutation stored in a list `current`. The `result` list is the final output.

The method first checks if the input array is empty, in which case it returns an empty list.

Then, it uses a for loop to iterate over the array from the start index to the end. For each element, it adds the element to the current permutation, performs a recursive call with `i + 1` as the new start index, and then removes the element from the current permutation.

When the method returns from the recursive call, it adds the current permutation to the output list.

