The algorithm used is the following:

1. Initialize the variable `sum` to `0`, which will represent the number of perfect square numbers.
2. While the target `n` is greater than `0`, do the following:
    1. Increment `sum` by `1`, so that it now represents the number of perfect square numbers needed to reach the current value of `n`.
    2. Subtract the current value of `sum` squared from `n` to update `n`.
3. Return `sum` as the minimum number of perfect square numbers needed to reach `n` .

The time complexity of this algorithm is `O(1)` since it is a constant-time operation to calculate the square of an integer.

The space complexity of this algorithm is also `O(1)` because no additional data structures are required to store the intermediate values needed to calculate the solution.

